      "section": "Mathematics",
      "text": "The value of the definite integral <span class=\"math\">∫₀^π/2 \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sin x + \\cos x} dx</span> is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "π/2",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "π/4",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "π",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "π/8",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "Shortcut: Use King's Property <span class=\"math\">I = ∫_a^b f(x)dx = ∫_a^b f(a+b-x)dx</span>. Here <span class=\"math\">f(π/2 - x) = \\cos x/(\\sin x + \\cos x)</span>. Add original <span class=\"math\">I</span> and transformed <span class=\"math\">I</span>: <span class=\"math\">2I = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} 1 dx = π/2 ⇒ I = π/4</span>. Full Solution: Let <span class=\"math\">I = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sin x + \\cos x} dx</span>. Applying the property, <span class=\"math\">I = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin(\\pi/2-x)}{\\sin(\\pi/2-x)+\\cos(\\pi/2-x)} dx = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\cos x}{\\cos x + \\sin x} dx</span>. Adding the two expressions for <span class=\"math\">I</span>: <span class=\"math\">2I = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin x + \\cos x}{\\sin x + \\cos x} dx = ∫_0^{\\pi/2} 1 dx = [x]_0^{\\pi/2} = \\pi/2</span>. Therefore, <span class=\"math\">I = \\pi/4</span>.",
